.TH SIMPLE_SHELL 1 "January 2026" "simple_shell" "Holberton Simple Shell"
.SH NAME
simple_shell \- a simple UNIX command interpreter

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B simple_shell
.br
Runs an interactive prompt.
.br
Also supports non-interactive mode when commands are provided via standard input.

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B simple_shell
is a minimal educational UNIX shell developed as part of the Holberton School
curriculum.

The shell reads commands from standard input, parses them into arguments, and
executes them by creating child processes.

The shell supports both interactive and non-interactive modes.

In interactive mode, the shell displays a prompt and waits for user input.
In non-interactive mode, commands are read from standard input without displaying
a prompt.

At end of file (EOF), the shell exits gracefully.

.SH OPERATION
On each iteration, the shell performs the following steps:

.IP "1." 3
Displays a prompt \fB($) \fR when running in interactive mode.
.IP "2." 3
Reads a line from standard input using
.BR getline (3).
.IP "3." 3
Removes the trailing newline character.
.IP "4." 3
Tokenizes the input line into arguments separated by spaces using
.BR strtok (3).
.IP "5." 3
Checks if the command is a builtin.
.IP "6." 3
If not a builtin, resolves the command path using the
.B PATH
environment variable.
.IP "7." 3
Creates a child process with
.BR fork (2),
executes the command with
.BR execve (2),
and waits for the child to terminate using
.BR wait (2)
or
.BR waitpid (2).

.SH BUILTINS
.TP
.B exit
Exit the shell.

.TP
.B env
Print the current environment variables.

.SH COMMAND SEARCH
If the command contains a slash ('/'), it is treated as an absolute or relative
path and executed directly using
.BR execve (2).

Otherwise, the shell searches for the command in each directory listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable. The directories are separated by colons ':'.

For each directory, the shell builds a candidate path of the form:
\fIdirectory/command\fR
and checks its existence using
.BR stat (2).

If no executable is found, an error message is printed to standard error using
the same format as
.BR sh (1).

.SH ENVIRONMENT
The shell inherits the environment of the parent process.

The environment is represented as a NULL-terminated array of strings in the form:
\fBname=value\fR.

Relevant environment variables include:

.TP
.B PATH
A colon-separated list of directories used to locate executable programs.

.TP
.B HOME
The home directory of the user (not used by builtins in this version).

.TP
.B LANG
May affect the behavior of executed programs.

.SH EXECUTION
The shell creates a new process using
.BR fork (2).

The child process executes the requested program using
.BR execve (2),
passing the argument vector and the environment.

If execution fails, an error message is printed and the child exits with a failure
status.

The parent process waits for the child to terminate before displaying the next
prompt.

.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell returns 0 on normal termination.

If a command is not found, an error message is printed. In a complete shell,
this situation typically corresponds to exit status 127.

.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP "\[bu]" 2
No handling of quotes, escape characters, or comments.
.IP "\[bu]"
No support for pipes, redirections, or logical operators.
.IP "\[bu]"
Basic tokenization using spaces only.
.IP "\[bu]"
Limited number of builtins.
.IP "\[bu]"
No job control or signal handling.

.SH EXAMPLES
Interactive mode:
.PP
.nf
$ ./simple_shell
($) /bin/ls
simple_shell.c main.c
($) exit
.fi

Non-interactive mode:
.PP
.nf
$ echo "/bin/ls" | ./simple_shell
simple_shell.c main.c
.fi

.SH FILES
No configuration files are used.
The shell reads commands exclusively from standard input.

.SH SEE ALSO
sh(1), execve(2), fork(2), wait(2), environ(7), strtok(3), getline(3)

